## 16. REAL-TIME DATA SYNCHRONIZATION

The hospital dashboard must stay synchronized across authorized users.

When a ward staff member updates a bed, other authorized users should see the change without manually refreshing the page.

Real-time updates should cover:

- Bed status.
- Bed availability.
- Ward occupancy.
- Admission queues.
- Bed requests.
- Transfers.
- Discharge coordination.
- Cleaning tasks.
- Operational issues.
- Notifications.
- Activity feeds.

### Technical requirements

- Subscribe only to data the authenticated user is authorized to access.
- Recalculate occupancy from authoritative database records.
- Use transactional updates for related state changes.
- Prevent duplicate event processing.
- Handle temporary connection loss.
- Retry failed connections with sensible backoff.
- Reconcile data after reconnection.
- Show last synchronization time.
- Display a stale-data warning when updates are overdue.
- Avoid presenting cached information as confirmed current availability.

Where possible, keep a central event history for investigating discrepancies.

The application should work across the hospital's authorized devices and network connections without requiring users to manually update each ward's dashboard.

