## 21. FUNCTIONAL ACCEPTANCE REQUIREMENTS

Do not consider the application complete until the following tests pass.

### Test 1: Bed availability

Given a ward with 30 operational beds and 24 occupied beds:

- Available beds must equal 6 if no beds are reserved, blocked, or otherwise unavailable.
- Occupancy must equal 80%.
- The dashboard must show consistent totals across all relevant pages.

### Test 2: New admission

When an authorized user completes an admission into an available bed:

- The admission is saved.
- The bed assignment is created.
- The bed status changes to occupied.
- Available bed counts decrease by one.
- Occupancy updates.
- Relevant authorized users receive an update.
- An audit event is recorded.

### Test 3: Duplicate allocation prevention

If two users attempt to assign the same bed simultaneously:

- Only one conflicting assignment may succeed.
- The other user receives a clear explanation.
- No duplicate active assignment is created.

### Test 4: Patient transfer

When a transfer is completed:

- The receiving ward confirms arrival.
- The active patient location updates.
- The previous assignment closes.
- The receiving assignment is recorded.
- Both wards receive relevant updates.
- The sending bed enters the appropriate readiness workflow.
- The event is audited.

### Test 5: Discharge

When a discharge is confirmed:

- The active patient assignment closes.
- The discharge record is saved.
- The bed does not become available prematurely.
- The cleaning or readiness workflow begins where required.
- The dashboard reflects the change accurately.

### Test 6: Urgent bed request

When a ward submits an urgent request:

- The request appears in the central queue.
- Authorized managers receive the configured alert.
- The request status can be updated.
- Partial fulfillment is tracked accurately.
- The request history is retained.

### Test 7: Ward status update

When a ward manager reports a bed shortage:

- The update appears in the operations feed.
- Relevant managers are notified.
- The issue can be assigned.
- Resolution is recorded.
- The update remains in the activity history.

### Test 8: Role-based permissions

A user assigned only to the cleaning team must not be able to:

- Approve admissions.
- Reassign patients.
- View unrestricted clinical records.
- Change user roles.
- Access unauthorized wards.

The backend must reject unauthorized requests even if a user attempts to bypass the interface.

### Test 9: Real-time updates

When one authorized user changes a bed's status:

- Other authorized dashboards update without a manual refresh.
- Occupancy and availability remain consistent.
- Connection failures are visible.
- Reconnection restores synchronization.

### Test 10: Reporting

For a selected reporting period:

- Totals reconcile with the underlying records.
- Occupancy formulas are documented.
- Filters work.
- Exported results match the authorized on-screen report.
- Patient information is excluded unless explicitly authorized.

### Test 11: Capacity exceptions

When occupied assignments exceed a ward's operational capacity:

- The system flags the ward.
- It does not hide the discrepancy.
- An authorized exception workflow is available.
- The decision is recorded.
- Management receives the configured alert.

### Test 12: Data persistence

After logout, browser refresh, or application restart:

- Saved records remain in the database.
- User permissions remain enforced.
- Historical activity remains available to authorized users.
- Unsaved actions are not falsely reported as completed.

